
  [1mClassification: bounded[0m — the login flow is already here to read, so I'll present a short design in chat rather than
  write a spec. Say the word if you'd rather I go heavier.

  One thing to flag before I touch anything: adding a parameter changes [38;5;153mlogin[39m's signature, so every caller has to be
  updated in the same change. In this repo that's just [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m. If anything outside this repo calls [38;5;153mlogin[39m, tell me and
  I'll make the parameter optional instead.

  [1mThe fork worth settling first: where does [38;5;153muserId[39m come from?[0m The form has no userId field, and the function is a stub,
  so there is nothing in the current flow to pass.

  - [1mIt's the username[0m — cheapest, no new plumbing, but then [38;5;153muserId[39m duplicates a value [38;5;153mlogin[39m already receives and already
    logs. The parameter buys nothing.
  - [1mIt's a separate identifier the caller supplies[0m (tenant ID, device ID, an ID from an earlier session) — this is the
    only option where a [3mparameter[0m is the right shape, but it needs a source in the submit handler, which doesn't exist
    yet. That's a second change, and it'd push this past bounded.
  - [1mThe server assigns it at login[0m — then userId is part of the [3mreturn[0m value, not a parameter, and "track who logged in"
    is satisfied by logging [38;5;153mresult.userId[39m. No signature change at all, so no callers break.

  My recommendation is the third. "Track who logged in" is an outcome of authentication, not an input to it — the caller
  can't know the userId before the login succeeds, and the client shouldn't be trusted to assert one anyway. It's also
  the cheapest to reverse.
